{
  "title": "Integer to Roman",
  "description": "Convert the integer `num` to its Roman numeral.\n\n| Symbol | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| `I` | 1 |\n| `V` | 5 |\n| `X` | 10 |\n| `L` | 50 |\n| `C` | 100 |\n| `D` | 500 |\n| `M` | 1000 |\n\nSymbols are normally written from largest to smallest and added together. Six subtractive pairs are used instead of repeating a symbol four times: `IV` (4), `IX` (9), `XL` (40), `XC` (90), `CD` (400) and `CM` (900).\n\n### Examples\n\n| `num` | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| `3` | `\"III\"` |\n| `58` | `\"LVIII\"` |\n| `1994` | `\"MCMXCIV\"` |\n\n### Constraints\n\n- `1 <= num <= 3999`\n- Inputs are passed as function arguments and the answer is returned from the function. Do not read from standard input or print the result.\n",
  "reference": "string int_to_roman(int num) {\n    const pair<int, const char*> parts[] = {\n        {1000, \"M\"}, {900, \"CM\"}, {500, \"D\"}, {400, \"CD\"}, {100, \"C\"}, {90, \"XC\"},\n        {50, \"L\"}, {40, \"XL\"}, {10, \"X\"}, {9, \"IX\"}, {5, \"V\"}, {4, \"IV\"}, {1, \"I\"},\n    };\n    string out;\n    for (const auto& [value, symbol] : parts) {\n        while (num >= value) {\n            out += symbol;\n            num -= value;\n        }\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n",
  "template": "string int_to_roman(int num) {\n    // Return the Roman numeral for num.\n    return \"\";\n}\n",
